ncludes some of the amazing  things in science that we've covered  whether it's you know the  discovery of the dna double helix or  insulin or antibiotics or the space race  but also  uh some places where we went wrong and  science went wrong too and covering  things that turned out to be  dangerous or long or unethical so i  encourage you to  stick with us and look forward we've got  a lot more coming as part of our  celebration  of our centennial  and i am hopeful and grateful because of  our great journalists and many others  around the country  um  we just had someone posted a comment  saying how wonderful it was that people  were able to move from physical to  virtual socialization and work  and soon concerts work school play dates  while providing social interaction  in new ways online that's true i think  at the time it didn't seem  like it would nearly the compensation  for not being together but i know that  um all that virtual communication last  winter really helped me get through this  and i know it did for many others so  um  i would like to say  thank you again to our amazing  journalists and the work they do and  thank you to our great audience and  science news readers online and in print  for  supporting us  and  um  anybody have a last comment you want to  say before we sign off  all right please stick with us and  um see what we do in our next hundred  years of science coverage  thank you all
 

